{
  "slots": {
    "question": [
      {
        "claim": "How does regional political climate moderate the relationship between political conservatism and COVID-19 vaccine hesitancy and uptake?",
        "quotes": [
          "The goal of the present research was to examine how political climate moderates the relationship between self-reported political conservatism and COVID-19 vaccine hesitancy and uptake. We collected online survey data from 683 participants between March 8 and April 19, 2023. Controlling for age, education, income, and race, there was an interaction between political conservatism and conservative political climate for both vaccine and booster hesitancy (β = .07, p = .03; β = .12, p < .001, respectively), such that liberals were less likely to be hesitant regardless of political climate. However, conservatives living in liberal political climates were less vaccine hesitant than their conservative counterparts living in conservative regions."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "central_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Conservative individuals are more likely to receive booster doses in liberal regions, indicating that regional political climate moderates the relationship between political conservatism and vaccine uptake.",
        "quotes": [
          "Most strikingly, for booster status the interaction between political orientation and political climate indicated that the most liberal participants were over 65% likely to get boosted regardless of climate. The most conservative participants in liberal climates were 60% likely to get boosted, while conservatives in conservative climates were less than 35% likely to get boosted (OR =.83, p = .026)."
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The interaction between political conservatism and conservative regional climate is significant for both vaccine and booster hesitancy, with the effect being stronger for more conservative individuals in conservative regions.",
        "quotes": [
          "As political conservatism increases vaccine hesitancy also increases, but this increase is even greater for individuals who live in a more conservative regional climate."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "evidence": [
      {
        "claim": "The study used a convenience sample of 683 U.S. participants recruited via online panels, with data collected from March 8 to April 19, 2023, and included measures of political conservatism, regional political climate (via 2020 election vote share), and vaccine/booster hesitancy.",
        "quotes": [
          "We collected online survey data from 683 participants between March 8 and April 19, 2023. Controlling for age, education, income, and race, there was an interaction between political conservatism and conservative political climate for both vaccine and booster hesitancy (β = .07, p = .03; β = .12, p < .001, respectively), such that liberals were less likely to be hesitant regardless of political climate. However, conservatives living in liberal political climates were less vaccine hesitant than their conservative counterparts living in conservative regions."
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study employed linear and logistic regression models to assess the interaction between political conservatism and regional political climate on vaccine and booster hesitancy and uptake, controlling for age, education, income, race, and perceived vaccine access.",
        "quotes": [
          "Multiple linear regression models were built to assess the association between political conservatism and conservative regional climate with vaccine hesitancy, controlling for age, gender, education, income, race, and perceived vaccine access. Ordinary least squares estimation was used with default standard errors. Model 1 included only control variables age, gender, education, income, race, and vaccine access as dependent variables. Model 2 included control variables and political conservatism and conservative regional climate. Model 3 included all variables from Model 2 and the interaction between political conservatism and conservative regional climate."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "claim": "Liberals were less likely to be hesitant regardless of political climate, and conservatives in liberal climates were less vaccine hesitant than conservatives in conservative climates.",
        "quotes": [
          "However, conservatives living in liberal political climates were less vaccine hesitant than their conservative counterparts living in conservative regions."
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The interaction between political conservatism and conservative regional climate was significant for booster status, with the most liberal participants over 65% likely to receive a booster regardless of climate, while conservatives in conservative climates were less than 35% likely to receive one.",
        "quotes": [
          "Most strikingly, for booster status the interaction between political orientation and political climate indicated that the most liberal participants were over 65% likely to get boosted regardless of climate. The most conservative participants in liberal climates were 60% likely to get boosted, while conservatives in conservative climates were less than 35% likely to get boosted (OR =.83, p = .026)."
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The interaction effect was significant for both vaccine and booster hesitancy, with a stronger effect observed in more conservative individuals living in conservative regions.",
        "quotes": [
          "As political conservatism increases vaccine hesitancy also increases, but this increase is even greater for individuals who live in a more conservative regional climate."
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Vaccine hesitancy was positively correlated with political conservatism and conservative regional climate, and negatively correlated with age, income, and education.",
        "quotes": [
          "Vaccine hesitancy was positively correlated with booster hesitancy (r = .84, p < .001), economic conservatism (r = .27, p < .001), social conservatism (r = .31, p < .001), political conservatism (r = .30, p < .001), and conservative regional climate (r = .20, p < .001); and negatively correlated with age (r = −.16, p < .001), income (r = −.17, p < .001), and education (r = −.21, p < .001)."
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The likelihood of receiving a booster decreased as political conservatism increased, and this effect was stronger in conservative regional climates.",
        "quotes": [
          "There was a significant interaction between political conservatism and conservative regional climate (OR =.83, p = .026)."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "scope_and_limits": [
      {
        "claim": "The study relies on self-reported data, which is susceptible to social desirability and recall bias, and the sample is limited to White and Black participants recruited through an online platform, which may not generalize to the broader U.S. population.",
        "quotes": [
          "Additionally, our sample was recruited through an online platform and only includes participants with access to this platform. Further, our sample includes only White and Black participants recruited through Qualtrics Panels, and may not generalize to broader populations in the United States. Finally, it should be noted that this data is cross-sectional and we cannot assume causal or directional associations."
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study uses 2020 presidential election vote share as a proxy for regional political climate, and does not formally assess the role of other regional factors such as population density or socioeconomic status.",
        "quotes": [
          "Because we used 2020 presidential election votes by zip code as a proxy for political climate, it must be noted that other zip code-based differences could be driving the associations noted in this paper. Regional factors (e.g., population density) may play a role in linking 2020 election votes to vaccine attitudes and behaviors."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "theoretical_positioning": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors position their work within the existing literature on political ideology and health behavior, extending prior research by examining the moderating role of regional political climate on individual political orientation effects.",
        "quotes": [
          "The present research provides the first assessment of the potential interaction between political orientation, political climate, and COVID vaccination."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "interlocutor_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "The paper attributes to prior research the finding that conservative political climate is associated with higher vaccine hesitancy, and then disputes or builds on this by showing that the effect is stronger for conservatives in conservative regions.",
        "quotes": [
          "Previous studies have focused on either political orientation or political climate. By looking at political orientation and region climate, we identify a novel and important finding, specifically that conservative individuals are more likely to received booster doses in liberal regions."
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
